1
00:02:45,599 --> 00:02:51,599
Love Comes To The Forest Folk . . . and to you, in one
of the world's greatest love stories!

2
00:05:31,199 --> 00:05:37,199
Bambi is a animation, drama, family film directed by David Hand.

3
00:08:16,799 --> 00:08:22,799
Bambi's tale unfolds from season to season as the young prince of
the forest learns about life, love, and friends.

4
00:11:02,399 --> 00:11:08,399
Young Bambi (voice) (uncredited), played by Donnie Dunagan.

5
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:54,000
Young Thumper (voice) (uncredited), played by Peter Behn.

6
00:16:33,599 --> 00:16:39,599
Young Flower (voice) (uncredited), played by Stan Alexander.

7
00:19:19,200 --> 00:19:25,200
Young Faline (voice) (uncredited), played by Cammie King.

8
00:22:04,799 --> 00:22:10,799
Friend Owl (voice) (uncredited), played by Will Wright.

9
00:24:50,399 --> 00:24:56,399
Adolescent Bambi (voice) (uncredited), played by Hardie Albright.

10
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:42,000
Bambi is a 1942 American animated coming-of-age drama film produced by Walt Disney and released by RKO
Radio Pictures, loosely based on Felix Salten's 1923 novel Bambi, a Life in the Woods.

11
00:30:21,599 --> 00:30:27,599
It was directed by David D.

12
00:33:07,199 --> 00:33:13,199
Hand, and a team of six sequence directors.

13
00:35:52,799 --> 00:35:58,799
The main characters are Bambi, a white-tailed deer; his parents (the Great Prince of the forest and his unnamed mother); his
friends Thumper (a pink-nosed cottontail rabbit) and Flower (a skunk); and his childhood friend and future mate, Faline.

14
00:38:38,400 --> 00:38:44,400
In the original book, Bambi was a roe deer, a species native to Europe, but Disney
decided to base the character on a mule deer from Arrowhead, California.

15
00:41:24,000 --> 00:41:30,000
Illustrator Maurice "Jake" Day convinced Disney that the mule deer had large "mule-like" ears and were more common to
western North America, but that the white-tail deer was more recognized throughout the United States.

16
00:44:09,599 --> 00:44:15,599
The film received three Academy Award nominations: Best Sound (Sam Slyfield), Best Song
(for "Love Is a Song" sung by Donald Novis) and Original Music Score.

17
00:46:55,199 --> 00:47:01,199
In June 2008, the American Film Institute presented a list of its "10 Top 10"—the best ten films in each of
ten classic American film genres—after polling over 1,500 people from the creative community.

18
00:49:40,799 --> 00:49:46,799
Bambi attained third in animation.

19
00:52:26,400 --> 00:52:32,400
In December 2011, the film was added to the National Film Registry of the Library of
Congress as being "culturally, historically and aesthetically significant".

20
00:55:12,000 --> 00:55:18,000
A follow-up, Bambi II, premiered in theaters in Argentina on January 26, 2006, before being
released as a direct-to-video title in the United States on February 7, 2006.

21
00:57:57,599 --> 00:58:03,599
In January 2020, it was announced that a photorealistic
computer-animated remake was set to be in development.

22
01:00:43,199 --> 01:00:49,199
However, on January 2026, it was reported that the
live-action remake film had been cancelled.

23
01:03:28,799 --> 01:03:34,799
The film explores themes of loss of loved one,
cartoon, forest, coming of age, best friend.

24
01:06:14,399 --> 01:06:20,399
The tone is emotional.
